description:
Found while building fz-sc8.18 (the landed File.Stream uses Range's `b | c` two-variable idiom to avoid it). Reproduces on b870f5a87 on interp and run. With

    @spec step([binary], {:cont, a} | {:halt, a} | {:suspend, a}, (binary, a) -> {:cont, a} | {:halt, a} | {:suspend, a}) :: {:done, a} | {:halted, a} | {:suspended, a, () -> any}
    @spec line_step([{binary, [binary]}], a, (binary, a) -> ...) :: {:done, a} | {:halted, a} | {:suspended, a, () -> any}

on a step/line_step reducer loop behind Enumerable.reduce, `r = Enum.reverse(%FS{lines: ["a", "b"]})` prints `["b", "a"]` and then `[h | t] = r` raises

    ** (MatchError) no match of right hand side value:
        ["b", "a"]

A value that is a two-element list fails a cons pattern, so the value's static type under the single-variable spec is not a list the match can see (the match is compiled as failing before the value is looked at). Elixir gives "b" and ["a"]. Full repro: session scratchpad p18/repro_single_type_variable_spec_on_reducer_loop.fz. Start by dumping types (`fz2 run --dump types=p`) for main's r and the activation returns of step/line_step under the spec, and compare with the two-variable spelling.
